  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhancing Resilience of drought and conflict affected communities of Borena and Bale zones of Oromia Region Ethiopia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Facilitated access to new sustainable and drought-resistant livelihood options for 9,464 herders (4,808 men and 4,656 women) from the Arero, Yabello, 
Dirre and Wachile woredas.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved food security, nutrition, and long-term resilience to climate extremes and other external threats of 13,494 individuals from the most vulnerable older female- and youth-headed pastoralist households in four woredas in the Borena area.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project will address the needs of economically active women, older women and youths, who otherwise would not 
have the opportunity to be engaged in making use of available livelihood options. They will be able to cover their food 
gaps and will start to have a say in the development program of the community at large. i.e. after getting support from 
the project, the beneficiaries managed to narrow their food gaps by engaging themselves in the productive packages as 
viable livelihood options. They develop skills and knowledge to run their own business and improved their social 
interactions.
The project will reach a total of 13,494 right holders in the targeted woredas through multidimensional intervention 
approach to save the lives and livelihoods of disaster affected community by creating access to diversified income. The 
project will rehabilitate the exising cooperatives for the local community to engage in viable business and provide them 
different capcity building trainings. Along with this the project will facilitate seed money to begin and initiate the 
proposed businesses. As a result, the targeted women will be able to feed their children with nutritious food and milk 
and able to restore their livelihoods. In general, the sum total of all these interventions will contribute towards building 
the resilience capacity of the right holders.</narrative>
  </description>
